\title{O1 - \code{\verb~|once~} (EVAL tabled from the second evaluation of a key on) on top of the machine's default switch set}
\taxon{Report}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\tag{performance}
\meta{origin}{docs/research/profiles/O1.md}
\meta{moved}{2026-11-24}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Experiment of 2026-11-24, 21:07Z - 23:27Z, agent O1. Scout X1 ([rep-X1](rep-X1)) built \code{\verb~|once~}: a tabling key of EVAL, \code{\verb~|(term, bindings under the node mask, ls')~}, gets a frame and a tabling record only from its SECOND evaluation in a session on (a one-bit visit filter outside the store). X1 measured it against the generic tier and against \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~}: reachability -27 \% / -29 \% warm check time (JVM, one worker). Since then the default set became \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect,bodies,pchk,match,joint,code,casetrees,lazycases,directrules,lazyeval,same~}. The question here: what is left for \code{\verb~|once~} on top of THAT set. Working files: \code{\verb~|work/o1/~} (\code{\verb~|README.md~} there). Nothing was published; \code{\verb~|rlvm-machine/~} was not edited.}

\subtree{
\title{0. Verdict: NO-GO}

\p{\code{\verb~|once~} does not pay on top of the default set. By the rule of the task - GO if it pays on Cedar-level corpora or Mathlib by warm wall clock, or if it pays on reachability without costing anything elsewhere - it fails both halves:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Not much is left for it.} Under the default set the tabling records of EVAL are 2.0 \% of all records on reachability, 4.9 \% on the Cedar slice, 7 - 8 \% on Init, Std and cedar, 12 - 13 \% on two Mathlib slices (under X1's base \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~}: 39 \%, 22 \%, 30 \% on the first three). The case-tree blocks and \code{\verb~|bodies~} already removed what X1 measured. The filter removes 58 - 90 \% of what remains: 2.0 \% (reachability) to 8.0 \% (Mathlib) of the records, no record probe at all, 4 - 9 \% of the run probes - for +0.4 \% (reachability) and +3.0 to +5.6 \% (everything else) ticks.}
\li{\strong{Warm wall clock per pass, JVM, 8 workers, check-only passes with the engines kept} (section 4; three processes per configuration, interleaved): reachability -1.8 \%, Cedar slice -0.1 \%, Mathlib slice +0.1 \%, cedar +1.7 \%, Init +3.1 \%, Std +5.7 \%. No pair of ranges is disjoint, and the two corpora that move most move the wrong way. Whole pipelined passes: Std +2.9 \%, cedar -0.9 \%.}
\li{\strong{JVM, one worker}: reachability +9.9 \%, Cedar slice +3.3 \% (ranges disjoint both times, no round with \code{\verb~|once~} ahead), Init +5.1 \%.}
\li{\strong{Native image} (one window each; run although the JVM says no-go, because the one-worker JVM row was a surprise): one worker: reachability -4.3 \% (ranges overlap), Cedar slice +1.5 \%; 8 workers: reachability +1.5 \%, Std +3.5 \% (ranges disjoint, no round with \code{\verb~|once~} ahead). Peak RSS on reachability: 577 -> 449 MiB with one worker, 937 -> 1,034 MiB with 8.}
\li{So on reachability, where X1 had -27 \%, the five readings are -4.5 \% and -1.8 \% (JVM, 8 workers, the trees of 21:08Z and 21:27Z), +9.9 \% (JVM, one worker), -4.3 \% (native, one worker), +1.5 \% (native, 8 workers): nothing that holds across platforms and worker counts. No Cedar-level corpus and no Mathlib slice gains anywhere; Std loses 3.5 \% on the native image (ranges disjoint) and 5.7 \% on the JVM with 8 workers, Init 3 - 5 \% on the JVM. That is not "pays on reachability without costing anything elsewhere".}
}

\p{It is correct as rebased: the gate's five parts, the hostile matrix and E1's exports are clean with it (section 3). As X1 wrote it, it was not: the filter depended on the host depth bound and failed convention equivalence (section 1, item 4); whoever takes X1's patch \code{\verb~|0001~} for another experiment should take \code{\verb~|work/o1/patches/~} instead.}
}

