}

\p{How to read it.}

\ul{
\li{A row is called different only when the two ranges are disjoint.}
\li{\strong{JVM, 8 workers, check only}: no row is different. Reachability -1.8 \% (all three rounds ahead, CPU +0.7 \%), Cedar slice -0.1 \%, Mathlib slice +0.1 \%, cedar +1.7 \%, Init +3.1 \%, Std +5.7 \% (CPU +5.3 \%; one \code{\verb~|default~} process, 1,072 ms, lies inside the \code{\verb~|once~} range 1,064 - 1,089 ms, the other two below it).}
\li{\strong{JVM, 8 workers, whole pipelined passes} (the owner's metric): Std +2.9 \%, cedar -0.9 \%: nothing.}
\li{\strong{JVM, one worker, check only}: \code{\verb~|once~} is slower on all three corpora that were run. Reachability 1,004 -> 1,104 ms (+9.9 \%, CPU +6.8 \%; the fastest of the 18 \code{\verb~|once~} passes after the second, 1,050 ms, is slower than 16 of the 18 \code{\verb~|default~} ones), the Cedar slice +3.3 \% (the ranges are disjoint by 1 ms; CPU +3.4 \%), Init +5.1 \% (no round ahead; one \code{\verb~|once~} process inside the default range). X1's -27 \% on reachability at one worker has turned into a loss: it removed 38 - 41 \% of the records then and removes 2 \% now.}
\li{\strong{Native image, one worker} (the same jars, \code{\verb~|-O3~}; no JIT): reachability -4.3 \% (1,351 -> 1,294 ms; all three rounds ahead, ranges overlapping by 8 ms); the Cedar slice +1.5 \%. So the 10 \% that the JVM loses on reachability with one worker are not in the algorithm (ticks +0.4 \%) and not on the native image, which points at the code C2 makes of the evaluator once \code{\verb~|firstVisit~} is hot. That is a reading, not a measurement: no compilation log was taken.}
\li{\strong{Native image, 8 workers, check only}: reachability +1.5 \% (479 -> 486 ms: nothing); Std 1,157 -> 1,198 ms, +3.5 \%, ranges disjoint, no round ahead, CPU +3.9 \%: the Cedar-level corpus loses on the arena's platform too, by about the ticks the switch adds there (+3.6 \%).}
\li{\strong{The tree of 21:08Z} (jar \code{\verb~|211ba5234be5~}, JVM, 8 workers, 40 passes, \code{\verb~|timing/table-main2108Z.md~}): reachability 331 -> 317 ms, -4.5 \%, ranges disjoint (327 324 343 against 315 315 320), CPU -3.7 \%; the Cedar slice 253 -> 258 ms, +2.3 \%, not different. The same window on the tree of 21:27Z gives -1.8 \% and -0.1 \%.}
\li{\strong{Warm-up.} With 8 workers a JVM is still getting faster after ten passes of 0.3 s. The first two windows (tree of 21:08Z; 10 passes on reachability and the Cedar slice, 8 on Init and the Mathlib slice) were too short: they showed -11 \% on the Cedar slice and +5.7 \% on Init, and the Cedar slice number did not survive 40 passes (\code{\verb~|timing/table-short-not-used.md~}; in no table here). The pass counts above were chosen after that; the last half of the passes of a process is the warmest state measured, not a proven steady state.}
\li{\strong{Peak RSS.} For the JVM rows it is the heap the process touched (\code{\verb~|-Xms = -Xmx~}; check-only passes make no collection between passes), not the engine's footprint. Whole passes (a collection between passes): Std 1,764 / 1,772 MiB, cedar 1,995 / 1,986 MiB: equal. The one JVM row where it differs with disjoint ranges is Std with 8 workers, check only (3,608 - 3,853 against 4,470 - 4,714 MiB); an untimed repeat with 12 passes and the collector log on gave 3,530 against 3,229 MiB, the other way round, with 12 young collections each: collector timing, not footprint. The native image (the heap grows on demand): reachability 577 -> 449 MiB with one worker (577, 577, 577 against 446, 446, 458) but 937 -> 1,034 MiB with 8 workers (918 - 953 against 981 - 1,117); Std with 8 workers 990 -> 976 MiB (ranges overlapping); the Cedar slice 242 -> 245 MiB. No footprint gain that holds. The engine's own accounting is the row "bytes in use at the resets" of section 2 (-2 to -6 \%).}
}
}

